  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening National Level Coordination and Collaboration for more effective Disaster Risk Reduction (DRR) Interventions in Bangladesh</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Bangladesh is one of the most climate-vulnerable countries as well as one of the most disaster-prone countries in the world. Due to its geophysical characteristics - a tropical, low-lying, riverine country with a broad deltaic plain - Bangladesh faces a complex set of rapid-onset natural hazards, ranging from floods, cyclones, landslides and storm surges; as well as chronic, slow-onset hazards, including drought, sea level rise, riverbank erosion and salinity intrusion. When combined with the socio-economic context of Bangladesh, characterized by widespread poverty and high population density forcing many people to live in areas highly vulnerable to natural hazards, these natural hazards lead to loss of life, land, homes and livelihoods, as well as widescale displacement and damage to infrastructure. This project aims at strengthening national coordination and collaboration for more effective Disaster Risk Reduction (DRR) interventions in Bangladesh.</narrative>
  </description>
